1
00:00:30,338 --> 00:00:31,381
This is going to be

2
00:00:31,381 --> 00:00:34,967
the most promising technology in human history.

3
00:00:35,134 --> 00:00:40,473
AI is capturing the essence of cognition. In 2024 and 2025,

4
00:00:40,598 --> 00:00:44,477
coding became possible, and now we're starting to find that agents

5
00:00:44,477 --> 00:00:48,940
that do things end to end in other areas of knowledge work are possible.

6
00:00:49,065 --> 00:00:53,486
And even more recently, we're starting to see that fundamental discoveries

7
00:00:53,486 --> 00:00:58,491
in areas like biology and mathematics and physics are becoming possible.

8
00:00:58,699 --> 00:01:03,204
If humans can use AI to cure cancer, an evil

9
00:01:03,204 --> 00:01:07,083
human can use AI to make a bioweapon or do a cyber attack,

10
00:01:07,542 --> 00:01:11,003
we cannot succeed if we don't address these risks.

11
00:01:11,087 --> 00:01:15,925
And we are determined to get this right, because this is why I and this is why

12
00:01:15,967 --> 00:01:19,512
many people who work at Anthropic, this is why we work on this technology.

13
00:01:26,936 --> 00:01:29,856
Intelligence is now something humans can build.

14
00:01:29,856 --> 00:01:32,817
The implications of this are profound.

15
00:01:32,817 --> 00:01:36,654
We believe it will make every person far more capable and expand the world's

16
00:01:36,654 --> 00:01:41,325
capacity for cognitive work, transforming every industry and every discipline.

17
00:01:41,325 --> 00:01:45,246
This enormous economic impact begins with frontier intelligence,

18
00:01:45,496 --> 00:01:48,916
with what Claude can do. In 2024,

19
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:53,504
Claude could answer a question, retrieve information, or summarize a document.

20
00:01:53,546 --> 00:01:55,423
By the end of 2025,

21
00:01:55,423 --> 00:01:58,176
Claude could do deep research and multi-step projects

22
00:01:58,176 --> 00:02:00,595
like refactoring an entire code base.

23
00:02:00,595 --> 00:02:06,100
The type of task that required hours or even days of human work. Today,

24
00:02:06,309 --> 00:02:09,687
Claude can take on complex, open ended projects

25
00:02:09,687 --> 00:02:13,483
that once took months or years of research, analysis

26
00:02:13,483 --> 00:02:17,570
and execution by many people and complete them in days.

27
00:02:17,904 --> 00:02:21,949
In the near future, we see potential for frontier AI to drive

28
00:02:21,949 --> 00:02:26,829
some of humanity's greatest breakthroughs and open entirely new markets.

29
00:02:27,163 --> 00:02:31,209
Our success thus far emerges from five core advantages.

30
00:02:31,542 --> 00:02:34,962
These are our mission and focus, talent and culture,

31
00:02:35,671 --> 00:02:38,049
frontier research and safety work,

32
00:02:38,049 --> 00:02:41,010
our approach to compute and our enterprise strategy.

33
00:02:41,010 --> 00:02:43,346
All of this begins with our mission.

34
00:02:43,346 --> 00:02:46,432
The responsible development and maintenance of advanced

35
00:02:46,432 --> 00:02:49,435
AI for the long term benefit of humanity.

36
00:02:49,685 --> 00:02:53,689
It's an organizing force and key to our focused execution.

37
00:02:53,773 --> 00:02:58,903
We manage a universe of finite resources: compute, talent, and research capacity.

38
00:02:59,153 --> 00:03:03,115
The mission is how we create clarity for the team and deploy those resources

39
00:03:03,115 --> 00:03:07,745
to maximum effect. At challenging moments that call for hard choices,

40
00:03:08,079 --> 00:03:10,414
our mission sharpens our decision making.

41
00:03:10,414 --> 00:03:14,377
It led us to deploy models like Mythos exclusively to cyber defenders.

42
00:03:14,544 --> 00:03:18,256
It also led us to commit to keep Claude free of advertising.

43
00:03:18,589 --> 00:03:23,010
This mission and focus continue to attract the very best talent in the industry.

44
00:03:23,302 --> 00:03:25,221
That's been true since our founding.

45
00:03:25,221 --> 00:03:28,266
Our seven co-founders include the originators of ideas

46
00:03:28,266 --> 00:03:31,477
that now make up the foundation of the AI industry.

47
00:03:31,727 --> 00:03:36,607
This concentration of top talent extends to all levels of the organization,

48
00:03:36,816 --> 00:03:40,820
and we lead the industry in attracting and retaining the brightest

49
00:03:40,820 --> 00:03:41,821
minds in the field.

50
00:03:48,869 --> 00:03:51,080
Why do I work at Anthropic?

51
00:03:51,080 --> 00:03:53,874
Why do I? Why do I work at Anthropic? Why do I work at Anthropic?

52
00:03:53,874 --> 00:03:56,752
Why do I work at Anthropic?

53
00:03:56,752 --> 00:03:59,463
Because I'm an optimist.

54
00:03:59,463 --> 00:04:01,799
I want a safer, better world for my family

55
00:04:01,799 --> 00:04:04,802
and for humanity.

56
00:04:05,678 --> 00:04:06,387
Three years

57
00:04:06,387 --> 00:04:09,432
ago, my older brother died of a brain tumor

58
00:04:09,432 --> 00:04:12,810
at the age of 42, which is the age that I am now.

59
00:04:13,227 --> 00:04:17,815
And I believe deeply that through our mission and this technology,

60
00:04:17,815 --> 00:04:21,152
there will be a situation where people like my brother

61
00:04:21,193 --> 00:04:24,155
don't have to die.

62
00:04:24,155 --> 00:04:26,574
Somebody has to carry the weight of getting this right.

63
00:04:26,574 --> 00:04:29,577
And I really didn't want to watch from the sidelines.

64
00:04:30,911 --> 00:04:34,373
People here ask, is this the right thing to do?

65
00:04:34,624 --> 00:04:36,876
Before they ask, can we do it?

66
00:04:38,711 --> 00:04:39,503
It's a rare

67
00:04:39,503 --> 00:04:44,091
combination of a group of people who care deeply about making AI go well,

68
00:04:44,091 --> 00:04:47,511
and also care deeply about competence,

69
00:04:47,678 --> 00:04:50,640
coherence, integrity, qualities

70
00:04:50,640 --> 00:04:53,934
that actually make an organization effective.

71
00:04:54,518 --> 00:04:58,564
These benefits and these risks, this light and this shade

72
00:04:58,731 --> 00:05:01,734
was precisely why I chose to come work at Anthropic.

73
00:05:06,614 --> 00:05:08,658
The work we do to make our models safe

74
00:05:08,658 --> 00:05:12,161
and reliable is the same work that makes them more capable.

75
00:05:12,328 --> 00:05:16,707
We care about useful intelligence, how effectively a model handles

76
00:05:16,707 --> 00:05:20,586
a real world task, and the value this delivers to customers.

77
00:05:20,795 --> 00:05:24,590
We're relentlessly focused on answering the question, what can Claude

78
00:05:24,632 --> 00:05:27,635
do for me that other models can't?

79
00:05:27,718 --> 00:05:31,055
Compute is the critical infrastructure that enables this work.

80
00:05:31,430 --> 00:05:34,934
We've built a differentiated advantage here, not just in how we build

81
00:05:34,934 --> 00:05:36,143
our compute capacity,

82
00:05:36,143 --> 00:05:38,354
but in how we make the most of this capacity

83
00:05:38,354 --> 00:05:41,023
through the discovery of compute multipliers.

84
00:05:41,023 --> 00:05:42,024
We are the leading

85
00:05:42,024 --> 00:05:45,653
AI lab that has been focused on building for the enterprise from day one.

86
00:05:45,778 --> 00:05:48,739
We train our frontier models to address knowledge work.

87
00:05:48,739 --> 00:05:52,952
As a result, our customers get the best of both capability and value.

88
00:05:53,577 --> 00:05:56,205
These customers have enabled our historic growth

89
00:05:56,205 --> 00:06:00,418
and they span industries across financial services, technology,

90
00:06:00,668 --> 00:06:04,839
pharma, healthcare, government, manufacturing, and more.

91
00:06:05,297 --> 00:06:08,801
And this is also where our public benefit mission and safety focus

92
00:06:08,801 --> 00:06:12,012
find natural alignment. Enterprises care that the frontier

93
00:06:12,012 --> 00:06:15,433
is safe, trustworthy and useful as much as we do.

94
00:06:15,599 --> 00:06:18,144
As frontier intelligence grows more capable,

95
00:06:18,144 --> 00:06:22,314
our dedicated focus on safety research becomes that much more important.

96
00:06:22,565 --> 00:06:26,402
It's critically important to our customers and necessary for realizing

97
00:06:26,444 --> 00:06:28,279
AI's full potential.

98
00:06:28,279 --> 00:06:31,449
And Anthropic is fully committed to doing this work with excellence.

99
00:06:31,615 --> 00:06:34,493
Our public benefit mission is what brought us to this point,

100
00:06:34,493 --> 00:06:37,788
and it will continue to shape our work, including at the governance level.

101
00:06:37,997 --> 00:06:41,959
In addition to our executive leadership and our board of directors, Anthropic

102
00:06:41,959 --> 00:06:45,212
is advised by the Long-Term Benefit Trust, an independent,

103
00:06:45,212 --> 00:06:48,424
financially disinterested body that selects a majority of our board.

104
00:06:48,799 --> 00:06:52,386
The LTBT's role is to help Anthropic plan for and fulfill

105
00:06:52,428 --> 00:06:54,430
our public benefit mission as we grow.

106
00:06:54,430 --> 00:06:58,642
We believe AI's future should be shaped and led by democratic societies.

107
00:06:58,851 --> 00:07:01,395
We want to be good partners in helping governments

108
00:07:01,395 --> 00:07:04,648
adapt to the changes that come with AI's rising capability.

109
00:07:04,899 --> 00:07:08,194
We do this at the executive level, through the work of our policy team

110
00:07:08,235 --> 00:07:11,238
and through efforts like the Anthropic Institute, which shares

111
00:07:11,238 --> 00:07:14,450
research on how frontier AI shapes how we live our lives.

112
00:07:15,034 --> 00:07:18,913
We are still early in what we believe will be the biggest transformation of the

113
00:07:18,913 --> 00:07:23,292
human experience in the modern age, not just the profound economic potential,

114
00:07:23,584 --> 00:07:27,713
but in all the ways in which we believe AI can help humanity flourish.

115
00:07:28,255 --> 00:07:30,800
From curing diseases to accelerating growth,

116
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:33,761
to helping people live more fulfilled and abundant lives.

117
00:07:36,138 --> 00:07:38,599
So if you think about our Claude model family,

118
00:07:38,599 --> 00:07:42,311
it's designed to deliver value across all the goals you might have.

119
00:07:42,478 --> 00:07:45,606
Whether you're optimizing for cost or speed

120
00:07:45,606 --> 00:07:48,609
or intelligence. At the top end,

121
00:07:48,734 --> 00:07:51,737
Mythos and Fable push the frontier forward.

122
00:07:51,779 --> 00:07:55,324
We see customers use it to harden cyber infrastructure

123
00:07:55,366 --> 00:07:58,786
or make new scientific discoveries. At the other end,

124
00:07:58,911 --> 00:08:02,957
we have models that are extremely capable and highly cost effective,

125
00:08:03,123 --> 00:08:07,670
and businesses can rely on them at scale for well-defined, routine tasks.

126
00:08:07,962 --> 00:08:10,840
A key thing we think about is how everyone

127
00:08:10,840 --> 00:08:13,801
can get the benefit of that whole spectrum of models.

128
00:08:13,843 --> 00:08:17,680
Because even as model capability increases exponentially,

129
00:08:17,888 --> 00:08:21,600
what most people do with the models doesn't change that fast.

130
00:08:22,184 --> 00:08:25,187
We think about three principles as we build.

131
00:08:25,479 --> 00:08:28,440
The first is meeting customers where they are.

132
00:08:29,108 --> 00:08:31,235
There's a few ways to use Claude.

133
00:08:31,235 --> 00:08:34,280
First is through our own applications, like Claude Code

134
00:08:34,280 --> 00:08:36,323
or the Claude desktop or mobile app.

135
00:08:36,323 --> 00:08:40,244
But Claude is also available on enterprise applications like Word and Excel.

136
00:08:40,661 --> 00:08:43,664
We go deep into verticals and try to understand

137
00:08:43,873 --> 00:08:48,752
what does an expert in this domain need to be successful immediately?

138
00:08:49,211 --> 00:08:52,882
For instance, if you're a lawyer, we have a Claude for Legal bundle,

139
00:08:52,882 --> 00:08:55,885
which connects directly to the tools you need.

140
00:08:56,051 --> 00:08:59,889
This is something we think about for every domain.

141
00:09:00,264 --> 00:09:02,933
We have bundles for healthcare, financial

142
00:09:02,933 --> 00:09:05,936
services, life sciences, and more.

143
00:09:06,020 --> 00:09:10,691
Our second principle is that we build models and products side by side.

144
00:09:10,816 --> 00:09:14,778
We develop products for tomorrow's models, not just today's.

145
00:09:15,195 --> 00:09:18,908
Claude Code is a great illustration of why that matters.

146
00:09:19,325 --> 00:09:22,828
A couple of years ago, we could see that our prerelease

147
00:09:22,828 --> 00:09:25,831
models were getting better at understanding code.

148
00:09:25,873 --> 00:09:28,959
Couldn't quite do it all yet, but we could see the trend line.

149
00:09:29,335 --> 00:09:32,212
We built a product around that thinking

150
00:09:32,212 --> 00:09:35,090
and Claude Code was launched just a few months later.

151
00:09:35,090 --> 00:09:39,053
That introduced people to an entirely new way of coding,

152
00:09:39,386 --> 00:09:42,056
and of understanding what AI can do.

153
00:09:42,056 --> 00:09:47,186
This flywheel between model and product is a durable advantage in how we build.

154
00:09:47,394 --> 00:09:48,854
The model gets better.

155
00:09:48,854 --> 00:09:51,482
We develop new features powered by those advancements.

156
00:09:51,482 --> 00:09:53,651
Then we train the models to get even better

157
00:09:53,651 --> 00:09:57,279
at what people are trying to do, which reinforces the whole system.

158
00:09:57,655 --> 00:10:00,199
And we do that in coding and beyond.

159
00:10:00,199 --> 00:10:01,951
Our third principle is that we

160
00:10:01,951 --> 00:10:05,371
close the gap between the frontier and customers day to day.

161
00:10:05,871 --> 00:10:09,959
Most people still think about their AI tools as a replacement for search

162
00:10:09,959 --> 00:10:13,170
or a simple Q&A tool, and it can be those things.

163
00:10:13,504 --> 00:10:17,049
But that understates the power of what people can really do

164
00:10:17,091 --> 00:10:18,759
with this intelligence.

165
00:10:18,759 --> 00:10:23,639
This is where product becomes a meaningful competitive differentiator.

166
00:10:23,681 --> 00:10:27,768
Without a strong product, the difference between growing intelligence

167
00:10:27,768 --> 00:10:30,813
and where users are just keeps compounding.

168
00:10:31,063 --> 00:10:35,192
We think the future will involve making Claude feel as simple

169
00:10:35,192 --> 00:10:40,030
and natural as possible, so people everywhere can just treat Claude

170
00:10:40,072 --> 00:10:44,618
the same way you treat a colleague and get high quality output every time.

171
00:10:44,910 --> 00:10:50,374
We think these three principles are how we bring frontier intelligence to everyone.

172
00:10:50,708 --> 00:10:54,712
This is what we mean when we say we want to give people superpowers.

173
00:10:54,920 --> 00:10:59,842
Claude starts by helping you do what you need to better and faster.

174
00:11:00,009 --> 00:11:01,218
Then it takes on

175
00:11:01,218 --> 00:11:05,055
the things you never would have tried because they were just too hard.

176
00:11:05,264 --> 00:11:09,852
And in the future, that means all of us doing what used to be impossible

177
00:11:10,227 --> 00:11:13,439
so we can all live better, fuller lives.

178
00:11:16,942 --> 00:11:19,236
When we discovered scaling laws in AI,

179
00:11:19,236 --> 00:11:22,906
we realized that AI capabilities could improve very fast.

180
00:11:23,157 --> 00:11:26,994
That's why we founded Anthropic to ensure that if AI became

181
00:11:26,994 --> 00:11:30,789
a transformational technology, it would be safe and beneficial.

182
00:11:30,873 --> 00:11:35,252
Our fundamental bet is that AI would make tremendous progress this decade.

183
00:11:35,377 --> 00:11:38,464
These transformational capabilities are why research

184
00:11:38,464 --> 00:11:41,467
and safety must advance together.

185
00:11:41,467 --> 00:11:44,803
It's the only way we'll realize the benefits of the technology.

186
00:11:45,012 --> 00:11:49,850
We assess our models across three dimensions: capability, efficiency,

187
00:11:50,100 --> 00:11:51,477
and safety.

188
00:11:51,477 --> 00:11:55,189
We optimize our models to be able to plan and complete complex work

189
00:11:55,230 --> 00:11:58,525
accurately, across hours and even days.

190
00:11:59,068 --> 00:12:01,737
A good example is Opus 5.5.

191
00:12:01,737 --> 00:12:05,949
One of our users was able to complete a 680K-line code migration

192
00:12:05,949 --> 00:12:06,950
in less than a day.

193
00:12:06,950 --> 00:12:10,287
This would have taken an engineering team weeks just a few years ago.

194
00:12:10,454 --> 00:12:14,708
We think our models are the very best at this long horizon work where accuracy

195
00:12:14,708 --> 00:12:15,959
really matters.

196
00:12:15,959 --> 00:12:18,962
The capability begins in the model training process.

197
00:12:19,129 --> 00:12:23,342
In the pre-training phase, a model learns from the totality of human knowledge.

198
00:12:23,425 --> 00:12:24,051
Text.

199
00:12:24,051 --> 00:12:27,054
Images, scientific papers, code.

200
00:12:27,304 --> 00:12:29,807
This phase is where it comes to understand

201
00:12:29,807 --> 00:12:32,810
fundamental human concepts and ideas.

202
00:12:33,185 --> 00:12:35,687
The second phase is reinforcement learning.

203
00:12:35,687 --> 00:12:38,690
This is where Claude learns to be helpful, honest, and harmless.

204
00:12:38,857 --> 00:12:41,819
It's where we draw out useful capabilities

205
00:12:41,819 --> 00:12:44,738
in coding, knowledge work, and science.

206
00:12:44,738 --> 00:12:48,200
As models have moved from conversational assistance to autonomous agents,

207
00:12:48,242 --> 00:12:49,827
reinforcement learning has evolved

208
00:12:49,827 --> 00:12:52,913
into one of the largest areas of research investment.

209
00:12:53,080 --> 00:12:55,457
One that really matters to our customers.

210
00:12:55,457 --> 00:12:58,127
In 2021, I wrote an internal memo

211
00:12:58,127 --> 00:13:03,215
outlining how our most likely product might be an aligned AI programmer.

212
00:13:03,340 --> 00:13:07,010
I wrote a lot of other things too, but this one turned out to be pretty accurate.

213
00:13:07,094 --> 00:13:10,347
Claude's training process is integral to Anthropic's mission.

214
00:13:10,514 --> 00:13:13,433
We need to understand how to train frontier intelligence

215
00:13:13,433 --> 00:13:14,768
and how to make it safe.

216
00:13:14,768 --> 00:13:17,271
And this is how we deliver the benefits of AI.

217
00:13:24,361 --> 00:13:25,487
We think that AI

218
00:13:25,487 --> 00:13:29,616
will help us reduce the time to do a clinical trial by 20 to 30%.

219
00:13:29,658 --> 00:13:33,787
If you get a drug to market 2 or 3 years sooner, those are either cures

220
00:13:33,871 --> 00:13:37,666
or treatments that patients that actually don't have solutions can have sooner.

221
00:13:37,749 --> 00:13:40,127
So we think it's actually a huge win for patients.

222
00:13:40,127 --> 00:13:43,130
And it's also a huge win for BMS.

223
00:13:43,380 --> 00:13:45,340
We chose Anthropic for a couple of reasons.

224
00:13:45,340 --> 00:13:48,177
One is they had the most mature enterprise capabilities.

225
00:13:48,177 --> 00:13:51,180
So it's one thing to have a chatbot that you can use.

226
00:13:51,180 --> 00:13:54,099
It's a whole different thing to actually put it in an environment

227
00:13:54,099 --> 00:13:56,143
where you're giving it access to some of the company's

228
00:13:56,143 --> 00:14:00,397
most confidential information, and you're able to do observability,

229
00:14:00,731 --> 00:14:04,902
manage the security, manage what the fine tune controls of what you give users.

230
00:14:04,902 --> 00:14:07,362
So there's like the enterprise controls that you'd often

231
00:14:07,362 --> 00:14:10,365
see that IT organizations really value a lot.

232
00:14:10,449 --> 00:14:13,452
We rolled it out across all 30,000 of our employees.

233
00:14:13,744 --> 00:14:14,953
This is the fastest,

234
00:14:14,953 --> 00:14:19,249
most well adopted AI tool in the history of what we rolled out.

235
00:14:19,333 --> 00:14:22,377
So now you can ask a very, very complicated question

236
00:14:22,544 --> 00:14:23,545
and it will go through.

237
00:14:23,545 --> 00:14:27,132
It will figure out which database or databases, because it can actually

238
00:14:27,132 --> 00:14:30,135
cross those databases, can be queried.

239
00:14:30,219 --> 00:14:31,553
And then it will produce a report

240
00:14:31,553 --> 00:14:34,890
that actually has the context of the epidemiology built right into it.

241
00:14:35,015 --> 00:14:39,353
So that takes you from what would have typically taken 1 to 2 months

242
00:14:39,353 --> 00:14:44,066
to get a simple question answered, to being able to get it in 4 to 5 minutes.

243
00:14:44,399 --> 00:14:48,654
And we're now finally at a stage both with high throughput computation,

244
00:14:48,654 --> 00:14:51,740
but also large language models where you're able to scale up

245
00:14:51,740 --> 00:14:55,202
your computation, your analytics to match the better scale of biology

246
00:14:55,202 --> 00:14:58,413
that can help the way that you inform and change the standard of care.

